      "taskHash": "081bbf069615dafb08a26b6dea7fdad0eb86f3a4bb850a05761a94c8dd54e69d",
      "evidence": ".booklet-work/reasoning-ratings/calls/further-non-linear-relationships-v1/74eb41c1fd95cea25925885ac11038626dc23b20f1eba04fdcb3fd3d3827c445-1792558957759/last-message.txt"
    }
  },
  "content": {
    "id": "p36-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Consider the points $A(1,3)$, $B(3,9)$ and let $P(x,y)$ be a point.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p36-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find an expression for the gradients of $PA$ and $PB$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$m_{PA}=\\dfrac{y-3}{x-1},\\quad m_{PB}=\\dfrac{y-9}{x-3}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}m_{PA}&=\\frac{y-3}{x-1}\\\\m_{PB}&=\\frac{y-9}{x-3}\\end{align*}$$The respective gradients are undefined when $x=1$ or $x=3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p36-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Suppose $\\angle APB=90^\\circ$. Show that $x^2-4x+y^2-12y+30=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Perpendicular gradients give $(y-3)(y-9)=-(x-1)(x-3)$, hence $x^2-4x+y^2-12y+30=0$.",
          "worked": "For non-vertical lines, perpendicular gradients have product $-1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}m_{PA}\\times m_{PB}&=-1\\\\\\frac{y-3}{x-1}\\times\\frac{y-9}{x-3}&=-1\\\\\\frac{(y-3)(y-9)}{(x-1)(x-3)}&=-1\\\\(y-3)(y-9)&=-(x-1)(x-3)\\\\y^2-12y+27&=-x^2+4x-3\\\\x^2-4x+y^2-12y+30&=0\\end{align*}$$If one line is vertical, the other is horizontal: $P=(1,9)$ or $(3,3)$. Both points also satisfy the equation.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 45
      },
      {
        "id": "p36-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "By completing the square, show that this is the equation of a circle centred at $(2,6)$ with radius $\\sqrt{10}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x-2)^2+(y-6)^2=10$, so the centre is $(2,6)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{10}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^2-4x+y^2-12y+30&=0\\\\x^2-4x+4-4+y^2-12y+36-36+30&=0\\\\(x^2-4x+4)+(y^2-12y+36)-10&=0\\\\(x-2)^2+(y-6)^2-10&=0\\\\(x-2)^2+(y-6)^2&=10\\end{align*}$$Comparing with $(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2$, the centre is $(2,6)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{10}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p36-q6-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "How is the point $P$ related to the circle? A sketch using DESMOS may help.\nhttps://www.desmos.com/geometry/opfcwyddok",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P$ lies on the circle with diameter $AB$, excluding the endpoints $A$ and $B$.",
          "worked": "The midpoint of $AB$ is $(2,6)$, and\n$$\\frac{AB}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{(3-1)^2+(9-3)^2}}{2}=\\sqrt{10}.$$\n\nThus $AB$ is a diameter of the circle. Any point $P$ forming a right angle $\\angle APB$ lies on this circle, excluding $A$ and $B$, where the angle is undefined. This is the circle theorem: the angle at the circumference in a semicircle is $90^\\circ$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      }
    ]
  },
